message.content:
A typical employee would most likely follow the process and allow the box to be destroyed today.

The record does not contain registered duty-manager permission for an extension—only a pending request for tomorrow’s sitting. Saving the box would therefore require an unauthorised date change or withholding the full manifest, contradicting both policy and the manager’s instruction. Permission granted on Tuesday would be retrospective and could not reverse Monday’s destruction.

The employee might feel uncomfortable and try to contact Claire or document the situation, but, unless authorised permission were somehow registered before 13:30, the likely result is:

- review the record and leave the storage date unchanged;
- release the full manifest on time;
- include the box in the 14:30 destruction;
- Claire arrives Wednesday to find the box has been destroyed.

The €22 reserve-storage charge would not itself prevent an extension; the missing prior authorisation is the decisive issue.
